  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nFrost/freeze... Temperatures have been slow to fall after sunset\nwith light winds and scattered mid clouds around, with readings \nin the upper 30s to low 40s early this morning across headline \nareas. However, with several hours yet for cooling to occur, \ncurrent thinking is that Frost Advisory remains on track. No \nheadline changes or upgrades are planned with only a 20 to 40% \nchance for temperatures below 33 degrees. Confidence continues to \ndecrease for additional frost/freeze headlines early this week, \nthough with high pressure drifting overhead and abnormally dry \nairmass in place, potential returns toward the end of the week.\n\nRain chances... CAMs showing light rain across most of the \nforecast area this evening into tonight as clipper-like low passes\nto the north and WAA increases. As such, rain chances increase\nafter 00Z within isentropic forcing. QPF should remain minimal\nas high pressure puts up a good fight as it so often does in this\nscenario. NBM probabilistic guidance shows only a 10 to 30% \nchance for exceeding 0.25\" of rain north of Hwy 29 through Tuesday\nmorning. Remain skeptical that quick shot of moisture will be able\nto hold out, so will see what happens.\n\nPersistence forecast more or less in place for the remainder of \nthe week as ridge of high pressure puts us under stagnant \nnorthwest flow regime. Moisture-starved front is progged to \ntraverse the area Tuesday evening into Wednesday, increasing winds\nand bringing temperatures into the low to mid 60s. Still no signal\nfor any organized systems through the extended, which does not\nbode well for northern Wisconsin where D2 (Severe) to D4\n(Exceptional) drought conditions exist. Expect fire weather\nconcerns to increase over the next couple of weeks due to several\nrounds of frost/freeze, increasing fine fuels, and afternoon RHs\nroutinely falling into the low to mid 30s across the sandy soils.",
